---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { VALUE } from '../../content/home';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Appliance Size and kWh Tools 2026: Who Publishes This Site';
const description = 'Appliance kWh in 2026: who publishes these appliance size and running cost tools, the ENERGY STAR and EIA data behind them, and what the site never sells.';
const total = Object.values(K.E.COUNTS).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Appliance kWh</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why these appliance tools exist</h2>
    <p>Someone replacing a refrigerator or fitting a washer into a closet asks two plain questions: which models fit, and what will they cost to run here. The pages that rank for them are written by stores and makers who want to sell one model, or by energy sites that quote an average wattage and a price per kWh several years old, often next to a form for solar panels or an electricity plan.</p>
    <p>{VALUE}</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds tools on public data. It is an independent site, not affiliated with ENERGY STAR, the EPA, the Department of Energy or the EIA. It sells no appliance, takes no commission, carries no store or affiliate link and asks for no contact details.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What it relies on</h2>
    <p>Seven ENERGY STAR certified product lists ({K.num(total)} models kept for the U.S. market, list of {K.day(K.UPDATED)}), table 5.6.A of the EIA Electric Power Monthly for {K.MONTH}, and the Department of Energy test procedures of 10 CFR part 430 for the hours behind each annual figure.</p>
    <h2>What it leaves out</h2>
    <p>Models without ENERGY STAR certification, prices of appliances, installation advice beyond the published sizes, and any recommendation of a brand. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every filter and the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains corrections.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for a size or a price that looks wrong. We answer questions about a figure or a source, not about which model to buy.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
